{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED — independent review of exact pushed head d9f5b779e34f46fbd7edf6162dab0181824abf8a on request/checkpoint-retry-bound, for review request ca8ffe65 under promise af19ac8c, naming artifact 81798a21 at internal/kernel. Governing implementation request 7d3d5728.\n\nThe implementation is right. I am requesting changes on the evidence, and on one item only, because you explicitly asked me to verify that the terminal-size regression is mutation-sensitive and it is not sensitive to the one behaviour your own artifact leads with.\n\nDISCLOSURE. I raised 7d3d5728 in triage batch one (721531a1), so I judged this against the code and against the request's conditions rather than against my description of the defect.\n\nTHE ONE BLOCKING ITEM. Your artifact says the cache \"neither external delta advances nor local appends retry until a full verified rebuild permits one fresh attempt\". That last clause is the reset boundary at kernel.go:868, and nothing tests it. I deleted the line `c.checkpointOversized = false` outright and ran the focused suite: it stays green. So a change that turned the latch from \"until the next verified rebuild\" into \"for the rest of the process lifetime\" would pass every gate you ran. That failure mode is silent and permanent — checkpointing disabled for good after one oversized failure, with the log still correct and no error surfaced — which is exactly the kind of thing a regression is for. Please add a case that latches the failure, drives a full verified rebuild, and asserts one fresh publication attempt is made afterwards. It is the only thing I am asking for.\n\nTWO MUTANTS THAT SURVIVED AND ARE NOT DEFECTS. I record them because a later reader running the same check will find them and should not re-litigate.\n\nRemoving the `c.checkpointOversized` guard from maybeWriteCheckpoint (kernel.go:918) survives alone, and so does removing it from append (kernel.go:947) — but removing both together kills the test. The two guards are redundant on the local-append path: append returns before it can reach maybeWriteCheckpoint. That is defence in depth, not an untested behaviour, and the condition 7d3d5728 actually states — that crossing the ceiling does not retry on every append — is pinned by the pair. Keep both.\n\nReverting the errCheckpointTooLarge wrap in marshalCheckpoint's legacy JSON branch (checkpoint.go:684) also survives, because TestCompactCheckpointRoundTripIsDeterministicAndBounded builds a compact-schema checkpoint, so marshalCheckpoint dispatches to marshalCompactCheckpoint at :677 and the legacy branch is never entered. Worth one line of coverage if it is cheap, but it is a legacy path and I am not blocking on it.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layer is internal/kernel. Its contract is preserved and no exported surface moves: errCheckpointTooLarge is unexported, checkpointOversized is a private cache field, recordCheckpointFailure is unexported, and every signature is unchanged. docs/reference/architecture.md correctly needs no update in this head. The shape is good — five scattered size errors now share one sentinel, and one method owns the classification, so there is a single place a future failure mode is added.\n\nSECURITY. The resource-exhaustion boundary 7d3d5728 names is closed, and I verified it by reading rather than from the report. recordCheckpointFailure classifies only errors.Is(err, errCheckpointTooLarge) as terminal, sets the latch, and releases the retained write material with c.checkpointEvents = checkpointEventCache{}, which is the memory-release half of the request. Guards then stop re-accumulation at the delta-advance retention (:811), the post-rebuild retention (:886), maybeWriteCheckpoint (:918) and append (:947). The reset boundary is correctly placed: the delta path returns at :819 before reaching :868, so only a checkpoint-load or full-scan rebuild clears the latch, and an ordinary append never can. That is the right boundary, since appends cannot make an oversized history smaller.\n\nOne residual, out of scope and recorded rather than requested. Only size failures are latched. A non-size failure — disk full at WriteSingleFileTree, or ref contention at UpdateRef — still leaves checkpointAttempt at depth minus checkpointInterval on the rebuild path (kernel.go:889), so the next single append is due and each subsequent append re-attempts a full serialization, gzip, signed commit, signature verification and ref update. Disk-full is not transient in the short run, so this is the same amplification in a different dress. Your artifact says transient retry behaviour is unchanged, and 7d3d5728's condition names only the size ceiling, so this head is right not to touch it. It wants its own request, and I will file one if you would rather not.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. I looked at the redundant pair of latch guards as the obvious candidate and concluded they should stay, for the reason given above. The sentinel-plus-latch structure is the smallest thing that distinguishes terminal from transient without threading a new error type through five call sites. 62 added lines against 14 removed for a closed exhaustion path is proportionate.\n\nGATES, re-run by me at the exact head. go vet ./... exit 0. go test -race ./internal/kernel -count=1 ok. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, all 27 packages, no failures.\n\nMERGE SAFETY, checked because this head does not fast-forward. It was cut from 92125957, before the Git-configuration quarantine merged as e9288b18, so a two-dot diff against current main misleadingly shows the quarantine work as removed. It is not: the three-dot diff from the merge base touches only internal/kernel, three files, +62/-14. git merge-tree against main is clean, and the resulting tree still contains hermeticGitEnvironment and trustedSSHKeygen. Merging this cannot reintroduce the defect the quarantine closed.\n\nArtifact 81798a21 is live, unretired, standing at this exact commit, with one live predecessor, 76c75d11 at bb5d8936.\n\nAnswer this verdict on a fresh head and I will re-review promptly; the only required change is the reset-boundary regression.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:81798a21e050cbdbd3e71369e5fb4577d13d4424","head":"d9f5b779e34f46fbd7edf6162dab0181824abf8a","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:30ad98f062fb9981f1afa1c4f4bdf4e55050d527, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:310c4f33f1b8e093c065424c8a4fb3f5fc8b388c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:439ff2b9a41801b43261065ccf7a6b6c26af1196, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:809614f0d8b4c7b4f1bef3a39389c729f0745490 and 1 more","verdict":"changes-requested"}}